1. Association
What is Association?
Association represents a relationship between two independent classes.
Describes “uses-a” relationship
Objects have their own lifecycle
Can be one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many

Interview one-liner
“Association defines a relationship where objects are loosely connected.”

Example (Association)
class Address {
    String city;

    Address(String city) {
        this.city = city;
    }
}

class Employee {
    String name;
    Address address;

    Employee(String name, Address address) {
        this.name = name;
        this.address = address;
    }

    void show() {
        System.out.println(name + " lives in " + address.city);
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Address addr = new Address("Hyderabad");
        Employee emp = new Employee("Sai", addr);
        emp.show();
    }
}

Output
Sai lives in Hyderabad
✔ Both Employee and Address exist independently
✔ Destroying one doesn’t affect the other
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Aggregation (Weak HAS-A Relationship)
What is Aggregation?
Aggregation is a special form of association that represents HAS-A relationship.
Child object can exist independently
Relationship is weak
Represented by reference field

Interview one-liner
“Aggregation represents a HAS-A relationship with independent lifecycles.”

Example (Aggregation)
class Department {
    String deptName;

    Department(String deptName) {
        this.deptName = deptName;
    }
}

class Employee {
    String name;
    Department department; // aggregation

    Employee(String name, Department department) {
        this.name = name;
        this.department = department;
    }

    void show() {
        System.out.println(name + " works in " + department.deptName);
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Department dept = new Department("IT");
        Employee emp = new Employee("Ram", dept);
        emp.show();
    }
}

Output
Ram works in IT
✔ Department can exist without Employee
✔ Employee can change department
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Composition (Strong HAS-A Relationship 🔥)
What is Composition?
Composition is a strong HAS-A relationship where:
Child object cannot exist independently
Lifecycle of child is bound to parent
Strong ownership

Interview one-liner
“Composition represents a strong HAS-A relationship with dependent lifecycles.”

Example (Composition)
class Engine {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Engine started");
    }
}

class Car {
    private final Engine engine = new Engine(); // composition

    void startCar() {
        engine.start();
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car car = new Car();
        car.startCar();
    }
}

Output
Engine started
Car started

✔ Engine cannot exist without Car
✔ When Car is destroyed → Engine is destroyed

4. Aggregation vs Composition (🔥 Very Important)
| Feature      | Aggregation         | Composition  |
| ------------ | ------------------- | ------------ |
| Relationship | HAS-A               | Strong HAS-A |
| Ownership    | Weak                | Strong       |
| Lifecycle    | Independent         | Dependent    |
| Example      | Employee–Department | Car–Engine   |
| Coupling     | Looser              | Tighter      |

5. Coupling
What is Coupling?
Coupling measures how strongly one class depends on another.
Tight coupling → Hard to change, test, maintain
Loose coupling → Flexible, scalable

Interview one-liner
“Coupling defines the degree of dependency between classes.”

Tight Coupling (Bad ❌)
class Engine {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Engine started");
    }
}

class Car {
    Engine engine = new Engine(); // tightly coupled

    void start() {
        engine.start();
    }
}

Problems:
Cannot replace Engine
Hard to test
No flexibility

Loose Coupling (Good ✅)
interface Engine {
    void start();
}

class PetrolEngine implements Engine {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Petrol engine started");
    }
}

class Car {
    private Engine engine;

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }

    void start() {
        engine.start();
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Engine engine = new PetrolEngine();
        Car car = new Car(engine);
        car.start();
    }
}

Output
Petrol engine started
✔ Easy to replace engine
✔ Testable & extensible
✔ Best practice

6. Coupling in Spring Boot (🔥 Interview Gold)
@Service
class OrderService {
    private final PaymentService paymentService;

    OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
        this.paymentService = paymentService;
    }
}

✔ Dependency Injection
✔ Interface-based design
✔ Loose coupling by default

7. Cohesion
What is Cohesion?
Cohesion measures how closely related responsibilities of a class are.
High cohesion → Single responsibility
Low cohesion → God class (bad design)

Interview one-liner
“Cohesion defines how focused a class is on a single responsibility.”

Low Cohesion (Bad ❌)
class UserService {
    void saveUser() {}
    void sendEmail() {}
    void generateReport() {}
}


Problems:
Multiple responsibilities
Hard to maintain
Violates SRP

High Cohesion (Good ✅)
class UserService {
    void saveUser() {
        System.out.println("User saved");
    }
}

class EmailService {
    void sendEmail() {
        System.out.println("Email sent");
    }
}

Output
User saved
Email sent

✔ Single responsibility
✔ Easy to modify & test

8. Coupling vs Cohesion (🔥 MUST-REMEMBER)
| Coupling               | Cohesion                   |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Inter-class dependency | Intra-class responsibility |
| Should be **LOW**      | Should be **HIGH**         |
| Design-level           | Class-level                |
| Affects flexibility    | Affects clarity            |

Golden Rule
“Low coupling and high cohesion lead to good design.”

